@jaimecbernardo: I deviated from the original proposal because I found that we need a more generic approach.

Let me share my thought process that you could better understand why I choose to implement it as it is. Sorry about not making this clear.

I started with the idea to add an UI toggle "Remove metadata which causes problems in some cases” and remove  metadata fields which contained malformed metadata in the past as described [here](https://github.com/microsoft/PowerToys/issues/9885#issuecomment-969355640). 

I analyzed the first image, identified the problematic metadata field, and removed it. I thought I would be done but discovered that other images in the comments of #9885 had different invalid metadata fields. So, I added more fields to my list of problematic metadata.

The problem is that in theory each field could contain malformed metadata. I needed a way to verify that I only remove the given field when its malformed. I discovered that `BitmapMetadata` throws an exception on `GetQuery` when you read an malformed metadata field.

It took me way longer than it should to realize that `BitmapMetadata` implements `IEnumerable` and that I could enumerate over all metadata. 
I altered my approach to generate the list of problematic metadata fields by iteration over all metadata and remove all fields where I get an exception while accessing a field with `GetQuery`.

I was convinced again that I solved everything. Unfortunately, I still got some errors with some images (exception on `encoder.Save(outputStream)`). I discovered that some metadata fields can be read without problems but will throw an exception on write either with `SetQuery` or ultimately while executing `encoder.Save(outputStream)` to persist the image to disk.

It became clear to me that the only approach is to rebuild the metadata object from the scratch and ignore all fields which throw an exception in either `GetQuery` or ` SetQuery`.

My final solution looks like this:

```cs
// read all metadata and build up metadata object from the scratch. Discard invalid (unreadable/unwritable) metadata.
// create new root metadata object
var newMetadata = new BitmapMetadata(metadata.Format);
// store temporary list of metadata excluding metadata fields which throw an exception on read (GetQuery)
var listOfMetadata = modifiableMetadata.GetListOfMetadata();
// iterate over all metadata and add it to the new metadata root
foreach (var (metadataPath, value) in listOfMetadata)
{
  // metadata is a nested data structure. Determine if it’s a node and not a leaf
  if (value is BitmapMetadata bitmapMetadata)
  {
    // create new metadata node
    var innerMetadata = new BitmapMetadata(bitmapMetadata.Format);

    // add the node to the metadata root object 
    newMetadata.SetQuerySafe(metadataPath, innerMetadata);
  }
  else
  {
    // it’s a leaf, add to metadata object. Ignore metadata which causes an exception on write (SetQuery)
    newMetadata.SetQuerySafe(metadataPath, value);
   }
 }
// replaces the old metadata root object with the newly created and validated metadata object
metadata = newMetadata;
```

To wrap things up:
-	We don’t remove any metadata for most of the images. We only remove metadata which will cause an exception on `encoder.Save(outputStream)`
- 	The approach is very generic and does not require manual maintenance of an ever-growing list of metadata fields which where problematic in the past
